  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSome light rain showers linger over northeastern South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota this morning. Storms yesterday produced a fairly \nextensive swath of 4\"-6\" in total precipitation (as of roughly 07Z) \nand MRMS estimates indicate that some localized areas may have \nreceived as much as 6\"-8\". While lingering showers are expec ted to \nbe fairly light in terms of rain rate, another 0.5\"-1\" of rain may \nfall through the morning hours today. Flooding concerns will remain \nwhere showers last this morning. \n\nAn upper-level trough will deepen over the eastern CONUS over the \nnext couple of days, which will shift the upper-level ridge axis \nover the region to become more positiiely tilted. The resulting \npattern leads to a thremal gradient over the forecast area over the \nweekend. Highs will range from slightly below normal (low 80s) east \nof the James River increasing to the 90s to perhaps low 100s west of \nthe Missouri River. Strong southerly winds from a low-level jet will \nalso be present this weekend. Saturday's winds will be a touch lower \nand more localized than Sunday's winds, gusting up to 20 to 30 miles \nper hour along and west of the Missouri River. Coverage will expand \nSunday afternoon, with gusts of 30 to 40 miles per hour possible \neverywhere along and west of the James River Valley. These winds \ncombined with the aforementioned heat will create some elevated fire \nweather concerns over central and north central South Dakota, \nparticularly on Sunday. With this latest forecast, the Grassland \nFire Danger will reach High to Very High that day for counties along \nthe Missouri River. Non-zero chance for a Red Flag Warning, but will \nlikely be highly dependent upon temperatures. \n\nThe next chances for rain come early next week due to the \ndevelopment of an upper-level trough and associated jet streak \nmoving over the region. This setup will bring a cold front across \nthe region, along which showers and storms may develop. There is \nstill a bit of uncertainty on the timing, but confidence is \nincreasing in the Monday evening to overnight Tuesday time frame \nhaving the best chances for rain (mainly across northeastern South \nDakota and western Minnesota). While still a bit far out to delve \ninto the specifics of a potential severe weather threat, but mid-\nrange model guidance continues to show probabilities of severe \nweather consistent with a Marginal Risk. \n\nBehind the cold front, expect a brief cooldown in temperatures. \nTuesday's and Wednesday's highs look to be about 5-10 degrees below \nnormal for a change, generally in the 70s across the board both \ndays. Beyond that point uncertainty grows drastically, but there are \nsome signals for a warmup back to at least near-normal for the end \nof the week.",
